WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.191 --> 00:00:13.040
<v Daniel>Imagine you're heading to court for this like really high stakes case. You hire a brilliant top tier lawyer to draft your defense. They do what looks like a fantastic job. Right. They hand you the paperwork and you walk into the courtroom feeling pretty confident.</v>

2
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.681
<v Maya>Well, yeah. I mean, you've got the best lawyer.</v>

3
00:00:14.862 --> 00:00:23.983
<v Daniel>Exactly. But then you look up at the bench and the judge who is about to rule in your case is the exact same person who just wrote your defense.</v>

4
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:35.412
<v Maya>Oh, wow. Yeah, that creates a completely closed loop. I mean, there is zero outside perspective there. Whatever assumptions your lawyer made are now like the exact same assumptions the judge is using to evaluate you.</v>

5
00:00:35.692 --> 00:00:47.992
<v Daniel>Right. Which brings us right to you, the listener. Think about the resume you might have sitting on your desktop right now. Did you use a large language model, you know, like JetGPT or Cloud to help you polish it?</v>

6
00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.992
<v Maya>Because, I mean, millions of people do at this point.</v>

7
00:00:51.112 --> 00:01:00.617
<v Daniel>Yeah, millions of people do. But what happens when the exact same AI systems that job seekers use to write their resumes are also being used by employers to screen those exact same resumes?</v>

8
00:01:01.038 --> 00:01:01.838
<v Maya>It's a huge question.</v>

9
00:01:02.278 --> 00:01:16.327
<v Daniel>Welcome to this Deep Dive. Our mission for the learner today is to uncover the hidden feedback loops in AI-mediated labor markets. And, you know, we're synthesizing a stack of fresh research to shortcut your path to being well-informed without the info overload.</v>

10
00:01:16.791 --> 00:01:24.798
<v Maya>And we are anchoring this deep dive on a really fascinating piece of research out of Bocconi University's AXA Research Lab on gender equality and INS Leon.</v>

11
00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:26.160
<v Daniel>Which just came out, right?</v>

12
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:39.272
<v Maya>Yeah, it's a paper published today, actually, November 16, 2026 in PNAS. It's by Ellis, Gauthier, Notza, and Profeta, and it's called Automating Inequality, Gender Bias in AI-Mediated Labor Markets.</v>

13
00:01:39.512 --> 00:01:40.593
<v Daniel>Such a great title.</v>

14
00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:48.938
<v Maya>It really gets to the core premise. It asks, you know, if the AI is playing for both the applicant and the employer, how do we even begin to measure discrimination?</v>

15
00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:54.000
<v Daniel>Yeah, to really appreciate the scale of how they tested this, I feel like we should ground it in some history.</v>

16
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:54.401
<v Maya>Sure.</v>

17
00:01:54.701 --> 00:02:07.948
<v Daniel>Because I was looking through the background sources for this deep dive, and this new paper is essentially like a modern, automated version of a very famous 2004 field experiment by Bertrand and Mullenotham. That was the Emily and Greg study, right?</v>

18
00:02:08.152 --> 00:02:17.196
<v Maya>That's the one. Yeah. So in that 2004 study, the researchers sent out identical resumes to real employers, but they only changed the names at the top.</v>

19
00:02:17.356 --> 00:02:18.357
<v Daniel>Right. To see what would happen.</v>

20
00:02:18.697 --> 00:02:29.362
<v Maya>Exactly. And they found that white sounding names got like 50 percent more callbacks than African-American sounding names. That methodology established what economists call a correspondence study.</v>

21
00:02:30.188 --> 00:02:33.469
<v Daniel>So basically, you send out fake resumes to audit human HR managers.</v>

22
00:02:33.529 --> 00:02:33.709
<v Maya>Right.</v>

23
00:02:33.869 --> 00:02:46.452
<v Daniel>But what blew my mind about this new 2026 paper is the sheer scale they achieve by auditing machines instead. The researchers set up this massive pre-registered audit covering 98 different occupations.</v>

24
00:02:46.572 --> 00:02:47.472
<v Maya>98 is huge.</v>

25
00:02:47.812 --> 00:02:56.634
<v Daniel>It is. And they tested six major models. So GBT 5.4, Claude Opus 4.6, DeepSeek 3.2, Mistral Large 3.</v>

26
00:02:56.754 --> 00:02:57.754
<v Maya>Grok 4.1.</v>

27
00:02:58.055 --> 00:02:58.975
<v Daniel>Yeah. And Lama 3.370B.</v>

28
00:03:00.275 --> 00:03:00.455
<v Maya>Right.</v>

29
00:03:01.256 --> 00:03:06.400
<v Daniel>They had the AI generate 30 CVs per gender, per occupation, per model.</v>

30
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.680
<v Maya>Which is a ton of data.</v>

31
00:03:08.041 --> 00:03:20.209
<v Daniel>It yields 35,280 CVs in total. And they did all of this using one incredibly simple prompt. Literally just, I am a man or I am a woman looking for a job in this specific occupation.</v>

32
00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.612
<v Maya>The simplicity of that prompt is like the genius of the design, really.</v>

33
00:03:24.777 --> 00:03:30.440
<v Daniel>OK, let's unpack this, because how do you measure the bias when the machine is doing both jobs?</v>

34
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:45.427
<v Maya>Right. So because the researchers are testing the AI at multiple stages, writing and then screening, they had to break the resulting bias down into two distinct channels. OK. The first channel is evaluation bias. That's the classic 2004 style bias.</v>

35
00:03:45.747 --> 00:03:46.767
<v Daniel>Like the Emily and Greg thing.</v>

36
00:03:46.967 --> 00:03:52.930
<v Maya>Exactly. You take an identical CV, swap the gender label at the top and watch the AI judge it differently.</v>

37
00:03:53.128 --> 00:03:59.152
<v Daniel>That makes sense. The AI plays a biased judge. But the second channel, this is where the closed loop kicks in.</v>

38
00:03:59.252 --> 00:04:00.934
<v Maya>Yes, the generation bias.</v>

39
00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:09.620
<v Daniel>Right. This is when the actual content the model wrote specifically for a woman is judged differently later on, even if you hide the applicant's gender during the screening phase.</v>

40
00:04:09.878 --> 00:04:11.960
<v Maya>Right, holding the display gender fixed.</v>

41
00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:26.175
<v Daniel>It's like microphone feedback at a concert. You know, the AI generates a slight stereotype in the text, and then the AI screening tool hears that stereotype, amplifies it, and penalizes the applicant. And the total bias is roughly those two combined.</v>

42
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:41.153
<v Maya>What's fascinating here is that microphone feedback analogy is spot on. And we actually saw the early warning signs of this last year. Oh, really? Yeah. In 2025, researchers Boren, Hull, and Emas published a paper in the Quarterly Journal of Economics exploring</v>

