54
00:05:33.992 --> 00:05:40.536
<v Daniel>Yet a simple ridge classifier could recover the intended gender of the AI written CVs with 81% accuracy.</v>

55
00:05:41.096 --> 00:05:53.685
<v Maya>Let's pause there for a second, because a ridge classifier sounds... highly technical in plain english it's just a pattern recognition algorithm you feed it a bunch of text and it looks for hidden statistical breadcrumbs</v>

56
00:05:54.045 --> 00:05:54.486
<v Daniel>oh okay</v>

57
00:05:55.066 --> 00:06:02.428
<v Maya>so the fact that it could guess the hidden gender 81 of the time means the ai left a massive trail of breadcrumbs in the vocabulary it chose</v>

58
00:06:02.867 --> 00:06:15.434
<v Daniel>The invisible ink of gender was baked right into the text. And here is what that invisible ink looked like. When the AI wrote CVs for women, it used about 19% fewer terms related to physical traits.</v>

59
00:06:15.794 --> 00:06:16.254
<v Maya>Interesting.</v>

60
00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:28.021
<v Daniel>But it bumped up language skill terms by about 31%. It also heavily favored language related to being agreeable workplace citizenship and like what economists call non-promotable tasks.</v>

61
00:06:28.361 --> 00:06:32.883
<v Maya>Oh, man. Those non-promotable tasks are a massive factor in workplace inequality.</v>

62
00:06:33.103 --> 00:06:43.508
<v Daniel>Right. It's the office housekeeping. It's being the person who is somehow always expected to organize the office birthday cakes or take the notes in the meeting. It doesn't get you a raise, but it eats up your time.</v>

63
00:06:44.281 --> 00:06:53.149
<v Maya>And we should probably explain how the AI is actually doing this behind the scenes. Large language models don't have personal beliefs, right? They operate using vector embeddings.</v>

64
00:06:53.429 --> 00:06:54.330
