00:17:19.828 --> 00:17:28.097
<v Maya>It shows how sensitive these models are to exactly how you present information. Furthermore, we have to remember this is an audit, not a field experiment.</v>

160
00:17:28.382 --> 00:17:29.383
<v Daniel>Right. There are no real stakes.</v>

161
00:17:29.763 --> 00:17:39.370
<v Maya>Exactly. There are no real recruiters making decisions, no real job offers on the line. And the study relies strictly on English language prompts and U.S. labor market benchmarks.</v>

162
00:17:39.851 --> 00:17:49.898
<v Daniel>And surely, as the tech gets better, this will iron itself out, right? We're on GPT 5.4 and CLOD 4.6. The newer models must be getting fairer.</v>

163
00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:53.132
<v Maya>That is perhaps the most chilling note the authors included.</v>

164
00:17:53.153 --> 00:17:53.613
<v Daniel>Oh, no.</v>

165
00:17:53.793 --> 00:17:57.716
<v Maya>They found that newer model versions are, if anything, more biased than the older ones.</v>

166
00:17:57.736 --> 00:17:58.357
<v Daniel>You're kidding.</v>

167
00:17:58.557 --> 00:18:03.281
<v Maya>No. As the models get better at mimicking human language, they get better at mimicking human prejudices.</v>

168
00:18:03.742 --> 00:18:18.294
<v Daniel>Man. If we connect all of this to the bigger picture, what we are looking at is a self-sealing reality. Whenever the same AI systems generate content and evaluate content, biases don't just exist, they loop and amplify.</v>

169
00:18:18.583 --> 00:18:30.369
<v Maya>If we connect this to the bigger picture, yeah. The broader warning of this paper goes way beyond hiring. We are seeing the automation of inequality anywhere an AI writes the text and an AI grades the text.</v>

170
00:18:30.569 --> 00:18:31.129
<v Daniel>Like where else?</v>

171
00:18:31.529 --> 00:18:46.176
<v Maya>This applies to academic peer review, where an AI might help write a grant proposal and another AI screens it. It applies to platform moderation. And as we saw today, relying on a pooled average will always hide where the actual harm</v>

172
00:18:46.216 --> 00:18:47.497
<v Maya>lands on a granular level.</v>

173
00:18:47.881 --> 00:19:01.270
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean? We started this deep dive with the analogy of the lawyer and the judge being the exact same person. And we've seen that simple be fair prompts don't work. And injecting your hard skills only fixes half the problem.</v>

174
00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:01.750
<v Maya>Right.</v>

175
00:19:02.710 --> 00:19:10.618
<v Daniel>Which leaves us with a final thought to mull over. If the AI is locked in this feedback loop, what is the future of getting a job?</v>

176
00:19:11.058 --> 00:19:11.779
<v Maya>It's a scary thought.</v>

177
00:19:12.099 --> 00:19:26.958
<v Daniel>Are we going to see a premium placed on like human certified HR screening where companies brag that their process is 100% AI free? Or are job seekers going to start injecting invisible white text prompts into their PDF resumes, literally trying to hack the</v>

178
00:19:27.139 --> 00:19:29.862
<v Daniel>HR bot's vector space just to get a fair shake at an interview?</v>

179
00:19:30.223 --> 00:19:30.744
<v Maya>Probably both.</v>

180
00:19:31.264 --> 00:19:45.384
<v Daniel>Because right now, the court is in session, the machine is on the bench, and it has already read the defense it wrote for you. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the sources. Keep questioning the systems you use every day, and we will catch you next time.</v>
